course: everyday
lesson: 12
title: Sounds that warn
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some sounds are made on purpose to warn you: sirens, car horns, smoke alarms and bike bells all say «pay attention now».
explain: Other warning sounds come from nature and animals: thunder means lightning is near, and a dog's growl means «back off».
explain: When you hear a warning sound, stop and work out where it comes from and what it means before you move.
explain: Example: a smoke alarm beeping loudly at night means get out at once — not hide, not look for toys, not finish your game.
explain: A family in Pennsylvania ignored a chirping smoke alarm with a flat battery for weeks; when a fire started at night the alarm stayed silent, and they woke late to a house full of smoke.
explain: A boy in Perth wore noise-cancelling headphones while walking behind a reversing truck; he did not hear its beeping and the driver stopped just in time.
fact: A siren means an emergency vehicle, such as an ambulance, a fire engine or a police car, is coming.
fact: When you hear a siren near a road, stay on the pavement or sidewalk and wait until the vehicle has passed.
fact: Drivers pull over to let emergency vehicles through, so traffic at a crossing can change suddenly.
fact: A car horn is a warning that a driver thinks someone may be in danger.
fact: A loud, repeating smoke alarm means there may be a fire: get out and stay out.
fact: A single short chirp every minute or so from a smoke alarm usually means its battery is low.
fact: A carbon monoxide alarm warns of a gas you cannot see or smell; when it sounds, everyone should go outside into fresh air.
fact: A dog that growls, shows its teeth or goes stiff is warning you to stay away.
fact: If a dog growls at you, stand still like a tree, do not stare into its eyes, and back away slowly once it calms.
fact: If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning, so get out of the water and go inside a building.
fact: In parts of the USA, outdoor sirens warn of tornadoes; when they sound, go inside to the lowest floor, away from windows.
fact: In a fire, call the emergency number from outside: 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland, 000 in Australia, 111 in New Zealand.
fact: A smoke alarm that does not work cannot wake you, and most deadly home fires happen at night while people sleep.
fact: Headphones can block the warning sounds of cars, bikes, horns and alarms.
exercise: What does a siren on the road mean? | An emergency vehicle is coming | A car alarm has gone off nearby | A shop is about to close | A train is leaving the station
exercise: You hear a siren just as you reach a crossing. What should you do? | Wait on the sidewalk until it has passed | Run across before the vehicle arrives | Cross, because all the cars will have stopped | Step into the road to see where it is
exercise: A smoke alarm beeps loudly over and over at night. What should you do? | Get out at once and stay out | Hide under the bed until it stops | Collect your favourite things, then go | Open the windows and wait inside
exercise: What does a short chirp from a smoke alarm every minute usually mean? | The battery is low | There is a big fire in the house | Someone pressed the test button | The alarm has just been fixed
exercise: A car honks as you step off the kerb. What does that mean? | The driver is warning you of danger | The driver is saying hello to you | The driver wants you to hurry across | The car's horn is being tested
exercise: A dog growls and shows its teeth at you. What should you do? | Stand still, look away, then back off slowly | Turn and run away as fast as you can | Stare into its eyes to show you are brave | Hold out your hand to let it sniff you
exercise: Why should you not run from a growling dog? | Running can make a dog chase you | Running makes a dog lose interest | Dogs cannot see things that move fast | Running tells the dog you want to play nicely
exercise: What does it mean if you can hear thunder? | Lightning is close enough to strike you | The storm is far away and harmless | The lightning has already finished | Rain will start in exactly one hour
exercise: Which alarm warns of a gas you cannot see or smell? | A carbon monoxide alarm | A burglar alarm by the door | An alarm clock | A car alarm that goes off in the street
exercise: A carbon monoxide alarm sounds at home. What should everyone do? | Go outside into fresh air | Go to bed and sleep it off | Light a candle to look for the gas | Switch the alarm off and stay inside
exercise: A girl in Kansas hears the outdoor tornado siren. What should she do? | Go inside to the lowest floor, away from windows | Go outside to watch for the tornado coming | Stand by a big window to see the sky and the clouds | Climb to the top floor of the house
exercise: Where should you call the emergency number from in a house fire? | From outside, once you are safe | From inside, before you leave | From the room where the fire is | From under your bed, while hiding
exercise: Which number would a boy in Dublin call in an emergency? | 999 | 911 | 000 | 111
exercise: Why do drivers pull over when they hear a siren? | To let the emergency vehicle get through | Because the siren can harm their engines | Because they must get out and help | To stop and watch the emergency
test: Sam is playing a game when the smoke alarm screams. His tablet is charging in another room. What should he do? | Leave the tablet and get out of the house | Fetch the tablet, then leave the house | Finish his level, then leave | Turn off the alarm and keep playing
test: Which sound tells you a dog wants you to stay away? | A low growl | A soft sleepy yawn | Quiet snoring | A playful yip while bowing down
test: You are swimming in an outdoor pool in Florida and hear thunder. What should you do? | Get out and go into a building | Stay in the water until you see lightning | Dive under the water to hide | Float on your back and wait
test: Which is true about a siren? | It warns that an emergency vehicle is coming | It means you must cross the road quickly | It only sounds when there is a fire | It means the traffic lights have broken
test: An ambulance with its siren on is coming, and the walk signal turns green. What is best? | Wait, because the ambulance may go through anyway | Cross at once because the signal is green | Cross slowly so the driver can see you | Wave at the driver and then cross
test: A neighbour's smoke alarm chirps once a minute and nothing smells of smoke. What is most likely? | A low battery | A big fire next door | A burglar in the house | A broken window
test: If a carbon monoxide alarm goes off, why can you not just sniff the air to check? | The gas has no smell at all | The gas smells just like flowers | The gas only smells in the morning | Your nose stops working near alarms
test: What does a car horn usually say? | Watch out, there may be danger | The driver is lost and needs help | The car is running out of fuel | The music in the car is too loud
test: Ava in Auckland sees smoke pouring from the house next door. Which number should she call? | 111 | 911 | 999 | 000
test: smoke alarm going off proper loud, what do i do | Get out and stay out | Look for the fire first | Hide under your bed | Turn it off and go back to sleep
test: alarm just chirps once every min, fire?? | No, probably the battery is low | Yes, get out right now | Yes, call the fire brigade | It means someone is at the door
test: siren coming, can i still cross | No, wait on the pavement until it passes | Yes, run across quickly | Yes, it will stop for you | Yes, if the little green man is showing on the light
test: can hear thunder but no rain yet, keep playing out? | No, go inside, lightning can reach you | Yes, until it starts raining | Yes, lightning only hits in rain | Yes, if you stay under a tree
test: smoke alarm keeps beeping every minute, so annoying, can i take the battery out | No, tell an adult so they can put in a new battery | Yes, take it out until morning, then put it back in again | Yes, put a sock over it | Yes, it is only a test beep
test: train horn blasting at the crossing, the gates are still going down | Stop and wait, a train is coming | Run across before the gates close | Look for the train first, then cross | Horns are just for drivers
test: Ava reckons a car alarm or horn is never about her. right? | No, a horn may be warning you to move | Yes, horns are only for other cars | Yes, it is just noise | Yes, unless it plays a song or keeps on beeping at her
test: my aunty's neighbour ignored the smoke alarm cos she thought it was toast and the whole house burned down | Real lesson: a loud repeating alarm means get out and stay out; the details may be added | Real lesson: toast always sets houses on fire | It proves smoke alarms go off for no reason | It is gossip, so alarms can be ignored
test: kid at school says if u can hear thunder ur totally safe cos the lightning's miles away | That is a wrong belief; if you can hear thunder you are close enough to be struck, so go inside | He is right, thunder means the storm has passed | He is right if you stand under a tall tree | He is right as long as it is not raining yet
test: Jacob's dad got pulled over for not moving for the ambulance, it's all over the group chat | Leave it alone; it is his family's business, and the real lesson is to make way for sirens | Spread it, since it is true and it is news | Spread it, so everyone teases Jacob at school | Spread it, and add that his dad got arrested
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a single brief chirp emitted by a smoke alarm at intervals of about one minute? | It ordinarily indicates that the battery is low | It indicates that a fire is in progress | It indicates that carbon monoxide is present | It indicates that the alarm is functioning perfectly
exam: It being given that a carbon monoxide alarm sounds within a dwelling, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | All occupants should proceed outside into fresh air | All occupants should remain indoors with the windows sealed | The alarm should be silenced and disregarded | The occupants should search for the source by smell
exam: Identify the course of action to be taken by a pedestrian upon hearing a siren in the vicinity of a road. | To remain upon the pavement until the emergency vehicle has passed | To cross the road with all possible speed | To stand in the road and wave to the driver | To follow the vehicle to observe the emergency
exam: Whereas a dog growls, bares its teeth and becomes rigid, determine the response recommended for a child. | To stand still, avoid staring into its eyes, and withdraw slowly once it calms | To run away as fast as possible while shouting | To stare directly into its eyes to show no fear | To reach out a hand and stroke it upon the head
